        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Santería</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1880 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Origin</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, in the cities of the west of the island, in the late 19th century, from the traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religion brought by enslaved West Africans, Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spiritism">Spiritism</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founder or early teachers</span><span class="info-stat-val">none: it has no single founder. It formed among Afro-Cuban communities, and a trained priesthood in the first houses of worship gave new initiates a degree of standardisation.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Scriptures</span><span class="info-stat-val">none: there is no key sacred text. Knowledge passes mostly by oral teaching and by taking part in rites; since at least the 20th century some initiates have kept notebooks (libretas), and the oricha are known through stories called patakís.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Followers today</span><span class="info-stat-val">The number of initiates is estimated to be in the high hundreds of thousands. For the early 21st century two scholars estimated that about 8% of Cubans, between 800,000 and 900,000 people, were initiates. The American Religious Identification Survey of 2001 estimated about 22,000 practitioners in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</span></div>
