    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Santería teaches a creator, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olorun">Olodumare</a>, and spirits called oricha who are equated with Roman Catholic saints; each person is believed to be born to one oricha, the owner of their head.</li><li>The religion formed among enslaved West Africans, most of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, and their descendants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, where Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> was the only religion the colonial government allowed.</li><li>It has no central authority and no key sacred text; each ilé, or house-temple, is largely autonomous, and membership requires initiation.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Santería's central public ritual is a drumming ceremony, the toque de santo, played on double-headed batá drums that are regarded as sacred; each ceremonial drum must be born from an existing one, so that drums form lineages.</p></aside>
    </section>

