      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1511</span><span class="rf-text">The first enslaved Africans arrive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Late 19th century</span><span class="rf-text">The first houses teaching Santería emerge in urban parts of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1898</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> becomes an independent republic whose constitution enshrines freedom of religion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1942</span><span class="rf-text">Rómula Lachatañeré's Manuel de santería is published, the first scholarly attempt to understand Santería as a religion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1959</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-revolution">Cuban Revolution</a> brings to power a government committed to state atheism, which takes a negative view of Santería</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1987</span><span class="rf-text">The Ooni of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> Olubuse II visits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, reinforcing links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1992</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>'s constitution is amended to declare the country secular rather than atheist</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1993</span><span class="rf-text">In Church of the Lukumi Babalu Aye v. City of Hialeah the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Supreme Court rules that animal cruelty laws aimed at Santería are unconstitutional</span></li></ol>
